Cuboidz wrote:
> 1) How thoroughly do you search each dungeon level before continuing
> to the next?

Personally I like the level to be quite fully discovered; i.e. I want
to have seen every room and want to know where the corridors between
the rooms are (which does not mean to have mapped every corridor; you
will get some feeling about where corridors will be - but take into
account that there may be boulders blocking your way in some unknown
part of a corridor). If I suspect a corridor I also explore it if it
would shorten my path from stair to stair or to some other important
point like an altar.

> Do you try to discover all secret rooms (including shops
> and vaults)?

Shops, yes, definitely; objects are the key option to make progress in
the game. Vaults, not, but there are exceptions; if you have a digging
tool and enough to eat (or found the vault-teleporter and have a wand
or digging tool) you'll get the money you need to buy items or to spend
for divine protection.

> Also, how many times do you search one spot before
> deciding nothing's there (using "n[number]s")?

I do that in cases if I need a passage and know there must be a secret
door in some wall (or at the end of some corridor). Usually I type n12s.
Sometimes I repeat it a sceond time.

Searching too much (and unnecessarily) means you're likely to starve.

> 2) Is "leveling" a good idea in nethack, e.g. running around until you
> see a monster to kill (to gain experience)?

Actually that are two questions.

Leveling up is only necessary until you reached level 14 to enter the
quest. It is also helpful to get the necessary slots to advance your
skills; specialized characters need less slots, spellcasters may want
some more (but beyond a certain point around experience level 14 you'll
get your levels by other means than killing monsters).

Killing monster is necessary. You will get death drops from them. You
will get your daily food from them. To gain experience? That's a side
effect :-) If you omit to kill them you need some pet(s) to kill them,
but pets happen to die, they are killed occasionally; so you need also
the maxHP's from the experience levels you get if _you_ kill monsters.

And every hostile monster that won't be killed will add to the present
monster population and make your life uncomfortable. Monsters are to be
killed.

> 3) Do you make the effort of picking up every single item, even if you
> don't need it,

I quite do that, but it's really not recommended. Better make stashes,
and hide potentially dangerous objects that you don't need yourself in
chests so that strolling monsters do not make use of them.

> in order to sell it in a shop - unless the load would

Sometimes I take some not so heavy objects (cloaks, bows/crossbows, or
spare mithril armor) to shops if I need some more gold to buy something
important.

> burden you, or course?

(I am not very qualified to give advice for unburdened play.) :-}

> Even more, do you run back for items you left
> because you couldn't carry any more items (for the same purpose of
> selling them)?

Not for selling them! But you may know that you need some item later,
so drop it at a stash and go back to your stash in time if you need it.

